  kicker="Buyer Segment"
  h1="Kids Cosmetics for Pharmacy & Drugstore Chains"
  lede="A pharmacy buyer is not buying a toy. The moment a product goes on the face, it is a cosmetic, and the rules that apply are the cosmetic rules — which is a different regime from the toy safety file most suppliers will send you."
  parent={{ href: '/industries/', label: 'Buyer Segments' }}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'Is a kids makeup set a toy or a cosmetic?',
      a: 'If it is applied to the skin or lips, it is a cosmetic in most jurisdictions, regardless of who it is marketed to. In the EU that means the Cosmetics Regulation and a CPNP notification; in the US it means FDA cosmetic requirements plus MoCRA registration where applicable. A toy safety certificate does not cover it. This is the single most common compliance gap we see in this category.',
    },
    {
      q: 'What does "washable" mean on these products?',
      a: 'In our range it means the formula is water-soluble, so it comes off with water or a mild soap rather than needing a remover. That is a genuine product difference and it is what parents ask about at the shelf. It is not the same claim as "hypoallergenic" or "dermatologically tested", and we do not use those words unless the specific item has been tested for them.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Who is the responsible person for a cosmetic in the EU?',
      a: 'A cosmetic placed on the EU market needs a responsible person established in the EU, named on the label, who holds the product information file. That is normally the importer or a specialist agency, not the Chinese factory. We can supply the manufacturing documentation and the formulation information the file needs, but we cannot act as the responsible person.',
    },
    {
      q: 'What MOQ applies to a pharmacy-grade order?',
      a: 'The cosmetic gift sets in our catalogue start between 60 and 120 pieces per design, depending on the case format. A chain will usually want a private label and a printed case, and that pushes the effective quantity up because the print run is separate from the toy run. Tell us the target shelf price and we will tell you which case formats can hit it.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Can you guarantee the products are non-toxic?',
      a: 'No factory should make that guarantee in the abstract. What we can do is tell you the formulation, the materials and the test documentation that exists for a specific item, and let your regulatory team or lab assess it. Claims such as "non-toxic" or "food-grade" are regulated claims in most markets and should be supported by a document, not a description.',
    },
  ]}
  related={[
    { href: '/collections/kids-makeup/', label: 'Kids makeup catalogue', note: '17 case formats from $5.72 to $23.03 FOB.' },
    { href: '/blog/private-label-kids-makeup-moq-labelling/', label: 'Private label makeup', note: 'Artwork, labelling and what the MOQ really covers.' },
    { href: '/tools/toy-certification-matrix/', label: 'Certification matrix', note: 'Where the cosmetic regime replaces the toy regime.' },
  ]}
>
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy mt-2 mb-4">The regime most buyers get wrong</h2>
  <p>
    A toy is assessed for mechanical hazard, flammability and migratable elements. A cosmetic is assessed for what happens
    when it sits on skin: the ingredients, the preservative system, the microbiological status, the labelling of the
    ingredient list. The two files are not interchangeable, and a supplier who sends a toy certificate for a lip gloss has
    not answered the question the pharmacy buyer asked.
  </p>
  <p>
    For a drugstore chain the consequence is practical: without the cosmetic documentation the product cannot be listed,
    regardless of how well it sells. That is why the compliance conversation has to happen before the commercial one in
    this category — and why we will tell you what a specific item does and does not have rather than assert a blanket
    claim across the range.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy mt-10 mb-4">What a chain buyer usually asks for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A named responsible person</strong> or a route to appoint one, and the label artwork that carries their
      details.</li>
    <li><strong>An ingredient list</strong> and the product information file, or the manufacturing documentation needed
      to build one.</li>
    <li><strong>Stability and microbiological testing</strong> appropriate to the formula and the packaging.</li>
    <li><strong>A washable, water-soluble formula</strong> — the parent-facing claim that actually drives repurchase in
      this category.</li>
    <li><strong>A case that survives a shelf.</strong> Aluminium and rigid cases photograph better and resist handling;
      card boxes dent.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy mt-10 mb-4">Choosing formats for a chain planogram</h2>
  <p>
    A chain buys a planogram, not a product: a small case at the entry price, a mid-size case for the gift occasion, and
    occasionally a premium trolley or vanity format for the seasonal aisle. Our range is built along that axis — the case
    format changes before the contents do — so a buyer can hold one formulation and three price points.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two practical notes. First, the aluminium and rigid cases are the ones that read as "real makeup" and they justify the
    higher shelf price; a card box at the same price invites a comparison the product will lose. Second, a trolley case
    sells in a seasonal display but not on a standard shelf, because it does not stack. Match the format to the fixture
    before you match it to the price.
  </p>

